illance of their own citizens, targeting political dissidents, targeting and suppressing the press—who somehow are able to sit on the sidelines and act as if it's the United States that has problems when it comes to surveillance and intelligence operations. And that's a pretty distorted view of what's going on out there. So I think that, as important and as necessary as this debate has been, it is also important to keep in mind that this has done unnecessary damage to U. S. intelligence capabilities and U. S. diplomacy. But I will leave it up to the courts and the Attorney General to weigh in publicly on the specifics of Mr. Snowden's case.","The question consists of 3 parts:

