588,The President's News Conference in Kapolei,"November 13, 2011",Barack Obama,https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/the-presidents-news-conference-kapolei,4,"Q. Thank you, Mr. President. Yesterday, in a speech before business leaders, you said that you want China to play by the rules. And then your staff later said that, in a bilateral meeting with President Hu, that you expressed that American business leaders are growing frustrated with the pace of change in China's economy. What rules is China not playing by? What specific steps do you need to see from China? And what punitive actions is your administration willing to take, as you said it would yesterday, if China does not play by the rules?","Well, first of all, I also said yesterday that we welcome the peaceful rise of. It is in America's interests to see succeed in lifting hundreds of millions of people out of poverty. can be a source of stability and help to underwrite international norms and codes of conduct. And so what we've done over the last 2 years is to try to develop a frank, consistent, open relationship and dialogue with, and it's yielded considerable benefits, for example, support for issues like. But what I've also said to Chinese leadership since I came into office is that when it comes to their economic practices, there are a range of things that they have done that disadvantage not just the United States, but a whole host of their trading partners and countries in the region. The most famous example is the issue of 's currency. Most economists estimate that the RMB is devalued by 20 to 25 percent. That means our exports to are that much more expensive and their imports into the United States are that much cheaper. Now, there's been slight improvement over the last year, partly because of U. S. pressure, but it hasn't been enough. And it's time for them to go ahead and move towards a market-based system for their currency. Now, we recognize they may not be able to do it overnight, but they